Experiment: Implementation of Subnetting in Cisco Packet Tracer

Aim
To design multiple subnets with a suitable number of hosts, assign static IP addresses, connect the subnets using a router, and simulate packet transmission to observe connectivity with and without a router.

Requirements
- Cisco Packet Tracer
- 1 router
- 2 switches
- 4 PCs
- Copper straight-through cables

IP Addressing Scheme
The network 192.168.1.0/24 is divided into two subnets using a /26 subnet mask (255.255.255.192).
Device	IP address	Subnet mask	Default gateway
PC0	192.168.1.2	255.255.255.192	192.168.1.1
PC1	192.168.1.3	255.255.255.192	192.168.1.1
Router G0/0	192.168.1.1	255.255.255.192	—
PC2	192.168.1.66	255.255.255.192	192.168.1.65
PC3	192.168.1.67	255.255.255.192	192.168.1.65
Router G0/1	192.168.1.65	255.255.255.192	—
- Subnet 1: 192.168.1.0/26 — usable hosts 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.62.
- Subnet 2: 192.168.1.64/26 — usable hosts 192.168.1.65 to 192.168.1.126.

Algorithm / Procedure
Part A: Design Multiple Subnets
1. Open Cisco Packet Tracer.
2. Place one router, two switches, and four PCs in the workspace.
3. Connect PC0 and PC1 to Switch0.
4. Connect PC2 and PC3 to Switch1.
5. Connect Switch0 to Router G0/0 and Switch1 to Router G0/1.
6. Use copper straight-through cables for the connections.
