Part B: Assign Static IP Addresses
1. Click each PC.
2. Select Desktop → IP Configuration.
3. Enter the IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway from the table above.
4. Configure the router interfaces using the CLI.
Router> enable
Router# configure terminal
Router(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
Router(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.192
Router(config-if)# no shutdown
Router(config-if)# exit
Router(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
Router(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.65 255.255.255.192
Router(config-if)# no shutdown
Router(config-if)# end
Router# show ip interface brief


If your router uses FastEthernet interfaces, select the corresponding interface names instead.
Part C: Test Connectivity With a Router
1. Wait for the router interfaces and switch links to become active.
2. Open PC0 → Desktop → Command Prompt.
3. Execute:
ping 192.168.1.3
ping 192.168.1.66
ping 192.168.1.67


4. Use Simulation Mode and Add Simple PDU to send a packet from PC0 to PC2.
5. Click Capture/Forward to observe the packet moving through Switch0, the router, and Switch1.
Expected output:
PC0> ping 192.168.1.66

Reply from 192.168.1.66: bytes=32
Reply from 192.168.1.66: bytes=32

Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0


The first ping may time out while ARP tables are being populated. Retry to verify connectivity.
Part D: Test Connectivity Without a Router
1. Disconnect the router from the switches or shut down its interfaces.
2. Keep PC0 and PC1 in Subnet 1 and PC2 and PC3 in Subnet 2.
3. Ping PC1 from PC0. The ping should succeed because both PCs are in the same subnet.
4. Ping PC2 from PC0. The ping should fail because no router is available to forward traffic between the subnets.
Expected output:
PC0> ping 192.168.1.3
Reply from 192.168.1.3: bytes=32

PC0> ping 192.168.1.66
Request timed out.


Observation Table
Test	Router connected	Router disconnected
PC0 → PC1 (same subnet)	Success	Success
PC2 → PC3 (same subnet)	Success	Success
PC0 → PC2 (different subnets)	Success	Failure
Communication between subnets	Possible	Not possible


Result
Multiple subnets were designed in Cisco Packet Tracer, and static IP addresses were assigned to all hosts. Packet transmission was simulated successfully. Communication between different subnets worked when a router was configured and failed when the router was disconnected, while communication within each subnet remained functional.
